# Backend Development Standards

> **Reference Implementation**: [backend/auth-service/](mdc:backend/auth-service/) - ALWAYS check auth service patterns first

## **CRITICAL: Service Structure**

### **Mandatory Directory Structure**

```
backend/service-name/
├── prisma/
│   ├── schema.prisma          # Database schema with audit patterns
│   └── migrations/            # Version-controlled migrations
├── controllers/               # Business logic handlers (NEVER inline functions)
├── middleware/                # Service-specific middleware
├── routes/                    # API route definitions
├── config/                    # Configuration modules
│   ├── database.js           # Prisma client setup
│   ├── redis.js              # Redis client setup
│   └── swagger.js            # OpenAPI documentation
├── validation/                # Joi validation schemas
├── .env.example              # Environment template
├── Dockerfile                # Container configuration
├── package.json              # Dependencies and scripts
└── server.js                 # Express app entry point
```

## **Controller Implementation**

### **CRITICAL: No Inline Functions in Routes**

**ALL business logic MUST be in controller methods - NEVER use inline functions in routes.**

```javascript
// ❌ FORBIDDEN: Inline functions in routes
router.get("/users/:id", auth.authenticate, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({
      where: { id: req.params.id },
    });
    // 20+ lines of business logic here - WRONG!
    res.json({ user });
  } catch (error) {
    // Error handling here - WRONG!
  }
});

// ✅ CORRECT: Controller methods only
router.get("/users/:id", auth.authenticate, UserController.getUser);
```

### **Controller Class Pattern**

```javascript
// controllers/entityController.js
const { PrismaClient } = require("@prisma/client");
const prisma = new PrismaClient();

class EntityController {
  static async create(req, res) {
    try {
      // 1. Input validation (handled by middleware)
      // 2. Business logic
      const entity = await prisma.entity.create({
        data: req.body,
        select: { id: true, name: true, createdAt: true },
      });

      // 3. MANDATORY: Audit logging
      await prisma.auditLog.create({
        data: {
          userId: req.user?.id,
          action: "CREATE",
          resource: "Entity",
          resourceId: entity.id,
          changes: req.body,
          ipAddress: req.ip,
          userAgent: req.get("User-Agent"),
        },
      });

      // 4. Standard response format
      res.status(201).json({
        status: "success",
        data: { entity },
        meta: {
          timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
          service: process.env.SERVICE_NAME,
        },
      });
    } catch (error) {
      logger.error("Entity creation error:", error);
      throw error;
    }
  }

  static async getById(req, res) {
    // Implementation...
  }

  // All methods MUST be static
}

module.exports = EntityController;
```

## **Database Patterns**

### **Prisma Schema Requirements**

**MANDATORY: Every service schema MUST include:**

```prisma
// Base fields for ALL models
model BaseEntity {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt
}

// REQUIRED: Audit logging model for every service
model AuditLog {
  id          String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId      String?  // User who performed action
  action      String   // CREATE, UPDATE, DELETE, etc.
  resource    String   // Table/model name
  resourceId  String?  // ID of affected resource
  changes     Json?    // What changed
  ipAddress   String?  // Request IP
  userAgent   String?  // Request user agent
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())

  @@map("audit_logs")
}
```

### **Database Per Service**

```javascript
// Each service MUST have its own database
// auth-service: logistics_auth
// user-service: logistics_users
// partner-service: logistics_partners

// Environment variable pattern
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://logistics:logistics123@localhost:5432/logistics_service_name"
```

## **API Standards**

### **Standard Response Format**

**ALL services MUST use this exact format:**

```javascript
// Success Response
{
  "status": "success",
  "data": { /* response data */ },
  "meta": {
    "timestamp": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
    "service": "service-name",
    "requestId": "req-uuid"
  }
}

// Error Response
{
  "status": "error",
  "error": {
    "code": "ERROR_CODE",
    "message": "Human-readable message",
    "details": {}
  },
  "meta": {
    "timestamp": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
    "service": "service-name"
  }
}
```

### **Authentication Patterns**

```javascript
// middleware/auth.js - Service-specific wrapper
const { authMiddleware } = require("../shared/lib/auth");

module.exports = {
  authenticate: authMiddleware.authenticate,
  requireRole: authMiddleware.requireRole,
  adminOnly: authMiddleware.adminOnly,
  // Service-specific extensions
};

// Usage in routes
router.post("/entities", 
  authMiddleware.authenticate,
  authMiddleware.requireRole(["admin", "operations"]),
  EntityController.create
);
```

### **Input Validation**

**MANDATORY: Joi validation for ALL endpoints**

```javascript
// validation/entitySchemas.js
const Joi = require("joi");

const createEntitySchema = Joi.object({
  name: Joi.string().min(2).max(100).required(),
  description: Joi.string().max(500).optional(),
  // Service-specific fields
});

// middleware/validate.js
const validate = (schema) => {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    const { error, value } = schema.validate(req.body);
    if (error) {
      return res.status(400).json({
        status: "error",
        error: {
          code: "VALIDATION_ERROR",
          message: error.details[0].message,
          details: error.details,
        },
      });
    }
    req.body = value; // Use validated data
    next();
  };
};
```

## **Server Configuration**

### **Standard Server.js Pattern**

```javascript
// server.js - Standard pattern from auth service
const express = require("express");
const cors = require("cors");
const helmet = require("helmet");
const swaggerUi = require("swagger-ui-express");
const logger = require("./shared/lib/logger");
const { errorHandler } = require("./middleware/errorHandler");
const swaggerSpecs = require("./config/swagger");

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 8002;

// Security middleware
app.use(helmet());
app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

// Health check endpoint - MANDATORY
app.get("/health", async (req, res) => {
  try {
    await prisma.$queryRaw`SELECT 1`;
    res.json({
      status: "healthy",
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      service: process.env.SERVICE_NAME,
      uptime: process.uptime(),
      database: "connected",
    });
  } catch (error) {
    res.status(503).json({
      status: "unhealthy",
      error: error.message,
    });
  }
});

// Swagger documentation - MANDATORY
app.use("/api-docs", swaggerUi.serve, swaggerUi.setup(swaggerSpecs));

// API routes
app.use("/api/entities", require("./routes/entities"));

// Error handling middleware - MANDATORY
app.use(errorHandler);

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`${process.env.SERVICE_NAME} running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

## **Docker Configuration**

### **Standard Dockerfile**

```dockerfile
FROM node:18-alpine

WORKDIR /app

# Install dependencies
RUN apk add --no-cache openssl
RUN npm install -g pnpm@8.15.1

# Copy source code
COPY . .

# Install dependencies and generate Prisma client
RUN pnpm install
RUN npx prisma generate

EXPOSE ${PORT:-8002}

HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --start-period=5s --retries=3 \
  CMD curl -f http://localhost:${PORT:-8002}/health || exit 1

CMD ["pnpm", "start"]
```

## **Service Checklist**

### **MANDATORY: Every Service Must Have**

- [ ] Controller methods only (NO inline functions in routes)
- [ ] Prisma ORM (NEVER raw SQL)
- [ ] Audit logging for all CRUD operations
- [ ] JWT authentication middleware
- [ ] Input validation with Joi
- [ ] Standard response format
- [ ] Health check endpoint
- [ ] Swagger API documentation
- [ ] Error handling middleware
- [ ] Docker configuration

### **🚨 MANDATORY VERIFICATION BEFORE TASK COMPLETION**

**EVERY backend task MUST include these verification steps:**

```bash
# 1. Check shared library imports
grep -r "require.*shared" backend/service-name/

# 2. Restart Docker service
docker-compose restart service-name

# 3. Verify service starts without errors
docker logs logistics-service-name --tail=20

# 4. Test health endpoint
curl http://localhost:PORT/health | jq .

# 5. Test main API endpoints
curl -X GET http://localhost:PORT/api/endpoint

# 6. Check for any MODULE_NOT_FOUND errors
docker logs logistics-service-name | grep "MODULE_NOT_FOUND"
```

**If ANY verification step fails, the task is NOT complete!**

## **🚨 CRITICAL: Docker Service Verification**

### **Rule 1: Service Verification After Changes**

**MANDATORY: After ANY changes to backend services, verify the service loads correctly using Docker exec:**

```bash
# Service verification pattern (replace {service-name} with actual service)
docker exec logistics-{service-name} node -e "
try {
  console.log('Loading server.js...');
  require('./server.js');
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error.message);
  console.error('Stack:', error.stack);
}
" 2>&1
```

**Service Container Names:**
- `logistics-auth-service` (main) or `logistics-auth-service-dev` (backend compose)
- `logistics-user-service` or `logistics-user-service-dev`
- `logistics-partner-service` or `logistics-partner-service-dev`
- `logistics-shipment-service` or `logistics-shipment-service-dev`
- `logistics-wallet-service` or `logistics-wallet-service-dev`
- `logistics-support-service` or `logistics-support-service-dev`
- `logistics-platform-service` or `logistics-platform-service-dev`
- `logistics-license-service`
- `logistics-api-gateway` or `logistics-api-gateway-dev`

**Example for user-service:**
```bash
docker exec logistics-user-service node -e "
try {
  console.log('Loading server.js...');
  require('./server.js');
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error.message);
  console.error('Stack:', error.stack);
}
" 2>&1
```

**This verification MUST be run:**
- After modifying `server.js` or entry point files
- After changing import paths or shared library usage
- After updating dependencies or package.json
- Before marking any backend task as complete

### **Rule 2: Database Commands Inside Docker Containers**

**CRITICAL: Database ports are NOT publicly exposed. ALL database and migration commands MUST run inside Docker containers.**

**❌ FORBIDDEN: Running migrations locally**
```bash
# WRONG - Will fail because ports aren't exposed
cd backend/user-service
npx prisma migrate dev
npx prisma studio
```

**✅ CORRECT: Run inside Docker container**
```bash
# Prisma migrations
docker exec logistics-user-service npx prisma migrate dev
docker exec logistics-user-service npx prisma migrate deploy
docker exec logistics-user-service npx prisma generate

# Prisma Studio (requires port mapping or exec)
docker exec logistics-user-service npx prisma studio

# Database seeding
docker exec logistics-user-service pnpm run db:seed

# Database queries
docker exec logistics-user-service npx prisma db push
docker exec logistics-user-service npx prisma db pull
```

**Use package.json monorepo commands when available:**
```bash
# Use these commands from root (they run inside containers)
pnpm run migrate:deploy:all
pnpm run db:seed
pnpm run db:seed:user
pnpm run prisma:license:deploy
```

### **Rule 3: Check Docker Logs Before Next Task**

**MANDATORY: ALWAYS check Docker logs before moving to the next task to verify service health.**

```bash
# Check logs for specific service
docker logs logistics-{service-name} --tail=50

# Check for errors specifically
docker logs logistics-{service-name} --tail=50 | grep -i error

# Check for MODULE_NOT_FOUND errors
docker logs logistics-{service-name} --tail=50 | grep "MODULE_NOT_FOUND"

# Follow logs in real-time
docker logs logistics-{service-name} -f

# Check all backend services
pnpm run logs:backend
```

**Log checking workflow:**
1. After making changes to a service
2. After restarting a Docker container
3. After running migrations or database commands
4. Before moving to the next task or marking current task complete
5. If service health check fails

**Common log patterns to check:**
- `Error:` - Any error messages
- `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` - Import path issues
- `Cannot find module` - Missing dependencies
- `ECONNREFUSED` - Database/Redis connection issues
- `EADDRINUSE` - Port conflicts
- `PrismaClientInitializationError` - Database connection problems

### **Rule 4: Use Monorepo Package.json Commands**

**CRITICAL: This is a PNPM monorepo. ALWAYS check `package.json` for available commands before running direct Docker commands.**

**Available Monorepo Commands (from root `package.json`):**

**Service Management:**
```bash
pnpm run dev:backend          # Start all backend services
pnpm run dev:user            # Start user service only
pnpm run dev:partner         # Start partner service only
pnpm run dev:wallet          # Start wallet service only
pnpm run dev:license         # Start license service only
pnpm run stop:backend        # Stop all backend services
pnpm run logs:backend        # View backend logs
pnpm run logs:user           # View user service logs
pnpm run logs:partner        # View partner service logs
```

**Database & Migrations:**
```bash
pnpm run migrate:deploy:all  # Deploy migrations for all services
pnpm run db:seed             # Seed all databases
pnpm run db:seed:user         # Seed user service database
pnpm run db:seed:partner      # Seed partner service database
pnpm run prisma:license:deploy  # Deploy license service migrations
```

**Health Checks:**
```bash
pnpm run health              # Check API gateway health
pnpm run health:wallet       # Check wallet service health
pnpm run health:license      # Check license service health
```

**Development Workflow:**
```bash
pnpm run setup:backend       # Setup backend environment
pnpm run fresh:backend       # Clean and fresh backend setup
pnpm run cleanup:backend     # Clean backend Docker resources
```

**When to use monorepo commands vs direct Docker:**
- ✅ **Use monorepo commands** for common operations (start, stop, logs, migrations)
- ✅ **Use direct Docker exec** for service verification, debugging, or one-off commands
- ✅ **Check package.json first** - if a command exists, use it instead of direct Docker

**Example workflow:**
```bash
# 1. Start services using monorepo command
pnpm run dev:backend

# 2. Check logs using monorepo command
pnpm run logs:user

# 3. Run migrations using monorepo command
pnpm run migrate:deploy:all

# 4. Verify service loads (direct Docker exec - no monorepo command for this)
docker exec logistics-user-service node -e "require('./server.js')"

# 5. Check logs before next task (monorepo command)
pnpm run logs:backend
```

## **NEVER DO**

- ❌ Use inline functions in routes (ALL logic in controllers)
- ❌ Write raw SQL queries (use Prisma only)
- ❌ Skip audit logging for CRUD operations
- ❌ Create endpoints without authentication
- ❌ Skip input validation
- ❌ Use custom response formats
- ❌ Skip error handling middleware
- ❌ **Mark task complete without Docker testing**
- ❌ **Ignore MODULE_NOT_FOUND or import errors**
- ❌ **Skip health endpoint verification**
- ❌ **Run database/migration commands locally** (ports not exposed - use Docker exec)
- ❌ **Skip service verification after changes** (must run Docker exec node -e test)
- ❌ **Move to next task without checking Docker logs**
- ❌ **Use direct Docker commands when monorepo commands exist** (check package.json first)

